notes:
Each template comes twice: Arduino C++, written for the Uno and Nano (template 8 is ESP32-only), and MicroPython for Raspberry Pi Pico boards and the ESP32. The C++ assumes a 5 V board with a 10-bit analogRead(): on an ESP32, pins 6 to 11 are the flash chip and analogRead() gives 12 bits (RNT ESP32 pinout, Arduino-ESP32 ADC); on a Pico the ADC reads against 3.3 V, not 5 V (Arduino-Pico analog). Pins are constants at the top: change them for your board, check [Board pinouts](#ref/pinouts), and keep off the ESP32's strapping and flash pins ([Microcontrollers](#ref/reference_microcontrollers)). In MicroPython, save the file as main.py to run it at every start-up; Ctrl-C at the REPL stops it (MicroPython boot, MicroPython REPL).

**Before you wire:**

- **Pin limits:** a 3.3 V board's pins must never see more than 3.3 V; ESP32 inputs are rated 3.6 V absolute max (MicroPython ESP32). An ATmega328P pin has a 40 mA absolute maximum, a damage limit and not a working figure: keep to about 20 mA (ATmega328P datasheet). Motors, relays and lamps go through a transistor or MOSFET.
- **Relays:** a common 5 V relay coil (Songle SRD-05VDC) draws about 71 mA (LCSC relay), more than a pin can give: use a relay module. Many modules switch on when their input goes LOW (RNT relay): set RELAY_ON to match yours. Wire the load through COM and NO, so a dead or reset board leaves it off. Check the contacts: a Songle SRD-05VDC-SL-C is rated 7 A at 28 V DC for a plain load and 3 A for a motor or other coil (Songle SRD datasheet); a bigger load needs a bigger relay.
- **Mains can kill.** Use these templates to switch low-voltage DC. Mains misuse can cause serious injury: if a relay must switch mains, keep everything unplugged while wiring (RNT relay), and get an electrician to do the mains side if one can be reached.
- **Grounds:** join the board's GND to the negative of anything it measures or switches.
- **Fuse:** put a fuse in the + wire of the load (Victron wiring), rated no higher than the wire, the load or the relay contacts can carry: a battery can push far more current into a short than the wire can take.

## 1. Is it alive? Blink and serial

Wiring: none; uses the board's LED. Open the serial monitor at 115,200 baud. On native-USB ESP32 boards, turn on USB CDC On Boot if nothing prints (Arduino-ESP32 troubleshooting).

```cpp
const int LED_PIN = LED_BUILTIN;  // ESP32 DevKit: 2
unsigned long n = 0;

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  Serial.begin(115200);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);
  delay(500);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  delay(500);
  Serial.print("alive ");
  Serial.println(n++);
}
```

```python
from machine import Pin
import time

LED_PIN = 25   # Pico: 25; Pico W and 2 W: "LED"; ESP32 DevKit: 2

led = Pin(LED_PIN, Pin.OUT)
n = 0
while True:
    led.toggle()
    print("alive", n)
    n += 1
    time.sleep(0.5)
```

The Pico W's LED hangs off its wireless chip, hence the name "LED" (Pico docs, MicroPython Pin). Many ESP32 DevKits have their LED on GPIO2 (RNT ESP32 pinout).

## 2. Button with debounce

Wiring: button from BTN_PIN to GND; the internal pull-up holds the pin high, so pressed reads LOW (Arduino pinMode). Contacts bounce: a change counts only after it has held for 50 ms, as in Arduino's own Debounce example (Arduino Debounce).

```cpp
const int BTN_PIN = 2;        // ESP32: use 14, GPIO2 is its LED
const int LED_PIN = LED_BUILTIN;
const unsigned long DEBOUNCE_MS = 50;
int stable = HIGH, last = HIGH;
unsigned long changedAt = 0;
bool ledOn = false;

void setup() {
  pinMode(BTN_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int r = digitalRead(BTN_PIN);
  if (r != last) { last = r; changedAt = millis(); }
  if (millis() - changedAt > DEBOUNCE_MS && r != stable) {
    stable = r;
    if (stable == LOW) {          // just pressed
      ledOn = !ledOn;
      digitalWrite(LED_PIN, ledOn);
    }
  }
}
```

```python
from machine import Pin
import time

BTN_PIN = 14    # button to GND
LED_PIN = 25    # see template 1
DEBOUNCE_MS = 50

btn = Pin(BTN_PIN, Pin.IN, Pin.PULL_UP)
led = Pin(LED_PIN, Pin.OUT)
stable = last = btn.value()
changed = time.ticks_ms()
while True:
    r = btn.value()
    if r != last:
        last, changed = r, time.ticks_ms()
    if time.ticks_diff(time.ticks_ms(), changed) > DEBOUNCE_MS and r != stable:
        stable = r
        if stable == 0:           # just pressed
            led.toggle()
    time.sleep_ms(1)
```

## 3. Battery voltage through a divider

Wiring: battery + → R1 (47 kΩ) → ADC pin → R2 (6.8 kΩ) → GND, battery − to board GND.

**Formula:** V_pin = V_bat × R2 ÷ (R1 + R2), so V_bat = V_pin × (R1 + R2) ÷ R2 (SparkFun dividers). Here V_pin = V_bat × 0.126: 15 V gives 1.9 V. Size your own with the [Voltage divider](#ref/divider) calculator.

**Never let the pin exceed what the ADC takes:**

| Board | ADC range |
|---|---|
| Uno, Nano (5 V) | 0 to 5 V, 10-bit (Arduino analogRead) |
| Pico (RP2040) | 0 to 3.3 V; never above its 3.3 V supply (RP2040 datasheet, MicroPython RP2) |
| ESP32 at 11 dB | Linear from about 0.15 to 2.45 V (MicroPython ESP32); 3.6 V absolute max |
| Bare ESP8266 | 0 to 1.0 V (MicroPython ESP8266); D1 mini A0: 3.2 V max (WEMOS D1 mini) |

The AVR's ADC wants a source of 10 kΩ or less (ATmega328P datasheet); 47k and 6.8k together look like 5.9 kΩ. The divider draws about 0.2 mA from a 12 V battery all the time.

```cpp
const int ADC_PIN = A0;
const float R1 = 47000.0, R2 = 6800.0;
const float VREF = 5.0;       // measure your board's 5 V pin and put it here
const float ADC_MAX = 1023.0; // 10-bit

float batteryVolts() {
  long sum = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) sum += analogRead(ADC_PIN);
  float vPin = sum / 16.0 * VREF / ADC_MAX;
  // ESP32: float vPin = analogReadMilliVolts(ADC_PIN) / 1000.0;
  return vPin * (R1 + R2) / R2;
}

void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); }

void loop() {
  Serial.print("Battery V: ");
  Serial.println(batteryVolts(), 2);
  delay(1000);
}
```

```python
from machine import ADC, Pin
import time

ADC_PIN = 26          # Pico GP26; ESP32: 34 (ADC1, works with Wi-Fi on)
R1, R2 = 47000, 6800

adc = ADC(Pin(ADC_PIN))   # ESP32: ADC(Pin(ADC_PIN), atten=ADC.ATTN_11DB)

def battery_v():
    raw = sum(adc.read_u16() for _ in range(16)) / 16
    v_pin = raw * 3.3 / 65535   # ESP32: v_pin = adc.read_uv() / 1e6
    return v_pin * (R1 + R2) / R2

while True:
    print("Battery V: %.2f" % battery_v())
    time.sleep(1)
```

On an ESP32 use analogReadMilliVolts() or read_uv(): both return calibrated millivolts or microvolts (Arduino-ESP32 ADC, MicroPython ESP32), but the datasheet still allows ±60 mV at 11 dB after calibration and ±6 % between chips without it (ESP32 datasheet): about ±0.5 V on a 12 V battery here. On any board, compare the printed volts with a multimeter on the battery and adjust the constants until they agree before you trust a cutoff.

## 4. Low-voltage disconnect

Wiring: divider as in template 3; relay module IN to RELAY_PIN; the relay's contacts in the load's + wire. The load drops off below CUTOFF and comes back only above the higher RECONNECT voltage; the gap (hysteresis) stops it clicking on and off. Take both from your battery's data: the example is a 12 V lead-acid battery, about a quarter charged at 12.0 V and full at 12.7 V resting (Battery University SoC); under load the reading sags below those figures ([Battery chemistry](#ref/reference_battery_chemistry)). Short dips, such as a motor starting, are ignored. Run the board from the battery through a 5 V buck converter, not straight from it: a Pico's VSYS takes 5.5 V at most (Pico datasheet).

```cpp
const int ADC_PIN = A0, RELAY_PIN = 7;
const float R1 = 47000.0, R2 = 6800.0, VREF = 5.0, ADC_MAX = 1023.0;  // Uno/Nano. Pico: VREF 3.3. ESP32: see template 3. Wrong values read high and never cut off
const float CUTOFF = 12.0, RECONNECT = 12.7;
const int RELAY_ON = LOW;     // LOW for most modules; HIGH for some
const int LOW_READS = 5;      // seconds below CUTOFF before cutting
bool loadOn = true;
int lowCount = 0;

float batteryVolts() {
  long sum = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) sum += analogRead(ADC_PIN);
  return sum / 16.0 * VREF / ADC_MAX * (R1 + R2) / R2;
}

void setup() { pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT); }

void loop() {
  float v = batteryVolts();
  lowCount = (v < CUTOFF) ? lowCount + 1 : 0;
  if (loadOn && lowCount >= LOW_READS) loadOn = false;
  if (!loadOn && v > RECONNECT) loadOn = true;
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, loadOn ? RELAY_ON : !RELAY_ON);
  delay(1000);
}
```

```python
from machine import ADC, Pin
import time

ADC_PIN = 26        # ESP32: 34
RELAY_PIN = 15      # ESP32: 26
R1, R2 = 47000, 6800
CUTOFF, RECONNECT = 12.0, 12.7
RELAY_ON = 0        # 0 for active-LOW modules, 1 for active-HIGH
LOW_READS = 5

adc = ADC(Pin(ADC_PIN))   # ESP32: add atten=ADC.ATTN_11DB
relay = Pin(RELAY_PIN, Pin.OUT, value=RELAY_ON)
load_on, low = True, 0

while True:
    raw = sum(adc.read_u16() for _ in range(16)) / 16
    v = raw * 3.3 / 65535 * (R1 + R2) / R2   # ESP32: adc.read_uv() / 1e6 * ...
    low = low + 1 if v < CUTOFF else 0
    if load_on and low >= LOW_READS:
        load_on = False
    elif not load_on and v > RECONNECT:
        load_on = True
    relay.value(RELAY_ON if load_on else 1 - RELAY_ON)
    time.sleep(1)
```

The board still draws from the battery when the load is off; the relay coil draws its 71 mA only while the load is on.

## 5. Thermostat relay with a DS18B20

Wiring: DS18B20 VDD to your board's own logic voltage (3.3 V on an ESP32 or Pico, 5 V on an Uno or Nano), GND to GND, DQ to ONE_WIRE_PIN, and 4.7 kΩ from DQ to VDD (DS18B20 datasheet). Never use 5 V on a 3.3 V board: the pull-up would put 5 V on its pin (MicroPython ESP32). Not on ESP32 GPIO12: the pull-up stops it booting. The sensor works from 3.0 to 5.5 V and reads −55 to +125 °C, ±0.5 °C from −10 to +85 °C; a 12-bit reading takes up to 750 ms. A reading of +85 °C right after power-up is its start-up value, not a temperature (DS18B20 datasheet). Arduino needs the OneWire and DallasTemperature libraries; −127 means no sensor (DallasTemperature). The heater comes on below HEAT_ON and goes off above HEAT_OFF; the example set points are yours to change. If the board hangs, the relay stays as it was, so put a thermal fuse or thermal cut-out in series with any heater (Wikipedia thermal cutoff). A missing sensor, or a data wire shorted to ground, turns the heater off (the MicroPython driver checks only the CRC, so the code also rejects an all-zero read, MicroPython ds18x20).

```cpp
#include <OneWire.h>
#include <DallasTemperature.h>

const int ONE_WIRE_PIN = 4, RELAY_PIN = 7;
const float HEAT_ON = 18.0, HEAT_OFF = 20.0;  // °C
const int RELAY_ON = LOW;
OneWire oneWire(ONE_WIRE_PIN);
DallasTemperature sensors(&oneWire);
bool heating = false;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  sensors.begin();
  pinMode(RELAY_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, !RELAY_ON);
}

void loop() {
  sensors.requestTemperatures();   // waits for the reading
  float t = sensors.getTempCByIndex(0);
  if (t == DEVICE_DISCONNECTED_C || t == 85.0) heating = false;
  else if (t < HEAT_ON) heating = true;
  else if (t > HEAT_OFF) heating = false;
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, heating ? RELAY_ON : !RELAY_ON);
  Serial.println(t);
  delay(2000);
}
```

```python
from machine import Pin
import onewire, ds18x20, time

ONE_WIRE_PIN = 4    # never ESP32 GPIO12
RELAY_PIN = 15      # ESP32: 26
HEAT_ON, HEAT_OFF = 18.0, 20.0   # °C
RELAY_ON = 0

ds = ds18x20.DS18X20(onewire.OneWire(Pin(ONE_WIRE_PIN)))
relay = Pin(RELAY_PIN, Pin.OUT, value=1 - RELAY_ON)
roms = ds.scan()
heating = False
while True:
    try:
        roms = roms or ds.scan()     # finds a sensor plugged in late
        ds.convert_temp()            # raises if nothing answers
        time.sleep_ms(750)
        t = ds.read_temp(roms[0])
        if not any(ds.buf):          # data wire stuck low reads all zeros
            t = None
    except Exception:                # no sensor or bad read
        t = None
    if t is None or t == 85.0:
        heating = False
    elif t < HEAT_ON:
        heating = True
    elif t > HEAT_OFF:
        heating = False
    relay.value(RELAY_ON if heating else 1 - RELAY_ON)
    print(t, heating)
    time.sleep(2)
```

## 6. PWM motor or LED dimming through a MOSFET

Wiring: pot ends to 3.3 V (5 V on an Uno) and GND, wiper to POT_PIN. PWM_PIN to the gate of an N-channel MOSFET made for logic-level drive, such as the IRLB8721, which Adafruit says a 3.3 V or 5 V pin can drive (Adafruit IRLB8721); its datasheet guarantees low resistance only from 4.5 V on the gate, so on a 3.3 V pin keep the current modest and check the MOSFET stays cool (IRLB8721 datasheet). MOSFET source to GND; load between its own + supply and the drain; supply − to board GND. Add 10 kΩ from gate to GND so the MOSFET stays off while the board starts up and its pins float (Arduino Forum MOSFET).

**Never run a motor straight from a pin:** it draws more than a pin can give and can damage the board (Adafruit motors). Put a diode across the motor, band (cathode) to the + supply, to catch the voltage spike when it switches off (SparkFun SIK motor).

Arduino's analogWrite takes 0 to 255 at about 490 Hz (980 Hz on Uno pins 5 and 6), on Uno and Nano pins 3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 11 (Arduino analogWrite); pins 5 and 6 may not switch fully off at the lowest settings, so use 9, 10 or 11 for a motor.

```cpp
const int PWM_PIN = 9, POT_PIN = A0;

void setup() { pinMode(PWM_PIN, OUTPUT); }

void loop() {
  int duty = analogRead(POT_PIN) / 4;   // 0-1023 to 0-255
  analogWrite(PWM_PIN, duty);
  delay(20);
}
```

```python
from machine import ADC, PWM, Pin
import time

PWM_PIN = 16     # to MOSFET gate; ESP32: 25
POT_PIN = 26     # pot wiper; ESP32: 34
FREQ = 1000      # Hz

pwm = PWM(Pin(PWM_PIN), freq=FREQ, duty_u16=0)
pot = ADC(Pin(POT_PIN))     # ESP32: add atten=ADC.ATTN_11DB
while True:
    pwm.duty_u16(pot.read_u16())   # both 0-65535
    time.sleep_ms(20)
```

## 7. I2C scanner

Wiring: module SDA and SCL to the board's, plus power and GND. I2C needs pull-up resistors (Arduino Wire). Uno: A4 is SDA, A5 SCL (Arduino Wire). MicroPython's I2C(0) defaults: Pico GP4 SDA, GP5 SCL (MicroPython RP2); ESP32 SDA 19, SCL 18 (MicroPython ESP32); Arduino's Wire.begin() on an ESP32 uses SDA 21, SCL 22 (Arduino-ESP32 I2C). Addresses 0 to 7 are reserved (Arduino Wire), and so are 120 to 127: MicroPython's scan() covers 8 to 119 (MicroPython I2C), and the C++ stops there too. A device that answers returns 0 from endTransmission (Arduino endTransmission).

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Wire.begin();
}

void loop() {
  int found = 0;
  for (byte addr = 8; addr < 120; addr++) {
    Wire.beginTransmission(addr);
    if (Wire.endTransmission() == 0) {
      Serial.print("Found 0x");
      if (addr < 16) Serial.print('0');
      Serial.println(addr, HEX);
      found++;
    }
  }
  Serial.print(found);
  Serial.println(" device(s)");
  delay(5000);
}
```

```python
from machine import I2C, Pin

SDA_PIN, SCL_PIN = 4, 5   # ESP32: 19, 18

i2c = I2C(0, sda=Pin(SDA_PIN), scl=Pin(SCL_PIN), freq=100000)
found = i2c.scan()
print(len(found), "device(s):", [hex(a) for a in found])
```

## 8. ESP32 deep sleep on a timer

For battery sensors: wake, read, report, sleep. Waking restarts the program from the top; Wi-Fi does not stay connected (ESP-IDF sleep). Most RAM is switched off (ESP-IDF sleep): keep counters in RTC memory with RTC_DATA_ATTR (Arduino-ESP32 sleep). The chip sleeps at about 10 µA (ESP32 datasheet), but a dev board draws more through its other parts (RNT deep sleep). To reflash a sleeping board, hold BOOT and press EN (Espressif boot ESP32). A pin keeps its level in deep sleep only if you hold it (gpio_hold_en with gpio_deep_sleep_hold_en) and is reset on wake (ESP-IDF GPIO), so do not leave a relay or MOSFET to a pin that sleeps.

```cpp
const uint64_t SLEEP_S = 600;      // 10 minutes
const uint64_t US_PER_S = 1000000ULL;
RTC_DATA_ATTR int bootCount = 0;   // kept through deep sleep

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  delay(500);
  bootCount++;
  Serial.printf("Wake %d, cause %d\n", bootCount, (int)esp_sleep_get_wakeup_cause());
  // read sensors and send data here
  esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(SLEEP_S * US_PER_S);   // microseconds
  Serial.flush();
  esp_deep_sleep_start();          // never returns
}

void loop() {}
```

```python
import machine, time

SLEEP_MS = 600_000   # 10 minutes
LED_PIN = 2

if machine.reset_cause() == machine.DEEPSLEEP_RESET:
    print("woke from deep sleep")
led = machine.Pin(LED_PIN, machine.Pin.OUT)
led.on(); time.sleep_ms(100); led.off()
# read sensors and send data here
time.sleep(5)        # a window to press Ctrl-C before it sleeps
machine.deepsleep(SLEEP_MS)
```

machine.deepsleep() takes milliseconds; reset_cause() tells a wake from a power-up (MicroPython ESP32). Leave the 5 s pause in: if main.py goes straight to sleep, the REPL is hard to reach.
